--TEST--
`is` against a `Core` namespace class — a name for static members that no value is ever one of — answers `false` wherever it is written, whole or inside a union, an intersection or an array element, and never refuses — `rule:types/type-test`, `rule:types/one-type-test`
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Ask {
    // `bool as string` renders `false` as the empty string, so a row of
    // predicates read through it silently loses its false columns.
    public static function yn(bool $b): string {
        if ($b) {
            return "y";
        }
        return "n";
    }
}

// `rule:types/type-test` is total: a target nothing is an instance of is a
// knowable answer rather than a meaningless question, so `Core\Str` — which
// carries static members and no instance ever — is `false` and not a
// diagnostic. The subject's own type decides nothing here.
mixed $m = 7;
string $s = "str";
echo Ask::yn($m is Core\Str), Ask::yn($s is Core\Str), "\n";

// The same question one type deeper. A union answers on its live member, so
// the settled one subtracts nothing; an intersection is settled whole, a value
// having to be an instance of every member at once.
echo Ask::yn($m is int|Core\Str), Ask::yn($m is Core\Str&Core\Arr), "\n";

// An array element is settled for that position alone, which is the row an
// empty array satisfies vacuously: there is no element to fail the walk.
mixed $filled = ["k" => "v"];
mixed $empty = [];
echo Ask::yn($filled is array<Core\Str>), Ask::yn($empty is array<Core\Str>), "\n";

// The roster is the descriptor's, not the spelling's: `Core\Time` is the
// namespace class and `Core\Time\Instant` is a class values are instances of,
// so the pair answers differently through one subject.
mixed $t = Core\Time::now();
echo Ask::yn($t is Core\Time\Instant), Ask::yn($t is Core\Time), "\n";
--EXPECT--
nn
yn
ny
yn
